Testing:
Tested connectivity using ping
ping “IP gateway” // is “default gateway” found in ipconfig /all

If it works - you can reach your router. + 
If it fails - problem with adapter, cable, or Wi-Fi

Test Wifi / Connection 
Ping a public IP (like Google’s DNS) // to know website public ip google it 
ping 8.8.8.8

If it works: internet connection is okay.
If it fails: network issue outside your PC.


Ping a domain name: DNS resolution (domain - IP) / Can I reach websites

		ping google.com
			If it works - DNS is okay.
If it fails - DNS problem (even if the internet works by IP).


Note:  8.8.8.8 vs nslookup google.com 

Nslookup google.com:
asks local DNS for site IP Website’s actual IP (from my local DNS) + DNS is working and gives site IP  

ping 8.8.8.8:
 DNS (Google) | raw connection test to internet

The DNS cache or adapter settings were preventing the system from resolving domain names. Flushing DNS and resetting the network stack cleared invalid entries and restored proper communication with DNS servers, allowing internet access. Updating drivers ensured the adapter functioned correctly.
